 "requested_at": "2026-11-12T19:52:26.712805Z",
 "text": "The clause is **not enforceable** as written. A tenancy in Ontario can end only in the ways the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 allows, such as a valid notice, a written agreement to terminate, or an order of the Landlord and Tenant Board. A term that makes the tenancy end automatically on the tenant taking a job outside Ontario is not one of those ways, so the Act overrides it. The clause also purports to require the tenant to vacate on that day, which the Act does not permit.\n\nA tenant who takes a job elsewhere still has ordinary options. A tenant with a periodic tenancy can give the notice the Act requires. A tenant under a fixed-term lease can try to reach a written agreement with the landlord to end it early, or can assign or sublet with the landlord's consent, as the Act provides.\n\nI am citing from memory and have not checked the consolidated text, so confirm the section numbers against the current Act before relying on them.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 19",
